  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Anomalously warm temperatures Thursday and \nFriday could approach daily record highs.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nAs a warm front lifts off to the north, the entire region has\nbecome positioned within the warm sector of an approaching low \npressure system. Despite mostly cloudy skies, strong S/SW flow \nshould advect anomalously warm temperatures into the area. \nTemperatures are expected to reach the 60s over most, if not \nall, of the area with the Bangor region likely to approach 70. \nFor reference, this is about 15 to 20 degrees above normal. Some\nuncertainty remains as to exactly how high temperatures will \nget as a result of persistent cloud cover but any areas that \nstay more clear will be even warmer, possibly reaching 70.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Additional rainfall expected Friday into the\nweekend, though amounts are still uncertain. The rainfall could\nhelp provide some drought relief.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nAn upper low approaching from the Great Lakes Friday will help \nsupport a developing coastal low, though the exact locations of the \ndeveloping low is still uncertain. The better chances of rain later \nFriday will occur across Downeast area. The upper low moves to the \neastern Great Lakes Friday night while the surface low lifts toward \nthe Gulf of Maine. Steadier rain should begin to expand northward \nacross the region Friday night. Uncertainty then develops Saturday \ninto Sunday regarding the track of the surface low. A track \ncloser to the Downeast coast would bring better rain chances to \nthe entire forecast area. A track across the Gulf of Maine to \nNova Scotia would keep the better rain chances across Downeast \nareas, with lesser rain totals northward across the remainder of\nthe forecast area. Precipitation totals Friday through Sunday \nwill be dependent on the eventual track of the low. A chance of \nrain showers could persist into Monday with an upper level \ndisturbance crossing the region.",
